window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "1.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 The question and the apparatus Imagine a source that sends one system towards a two-outcome detector. A screen displays 53 results labelled A and 47 labelled B. Does this establish that A is more likely? Before calculating, ask what counted as a trial. Were missing events discarded? Was the source stable? Did the two detectors have equal efficiencies? A list of numbers becomes evidence only when these operational details are attached. Separate preparation, transformation, and measurement. Preparation specifies what is sent in; transformation describes the controlled operation; measurement specifies the possible records. Repeat the same procedure to estimate probabilities. A quantum state is a tool for predicting those probabilities, not a photograph of the object between observations. Different explanations may fit the same finite dataset. Our first apparatus is intentionally simple: a source, a selectable probability model, and two counters. In the browser, a seeded random-number generator supplies events. It has no quantum hardware behind it. Replaying the same seed reproduces the same sequence, which is useful for debugging and makes the distinction between a model and evidence concrete."}, {"title": "1.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Build a prediction For independent trials with a constant probability p of A, the count k follows a binomial distribution. Its mean is Np and its standard deviation is √(Np(1−p)). Dividing by N gives the estimated proportion and its approximate standard error: \\(\\hat p=k/N\\), \\(\\mathrm{SE}\\simeq\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)/N}\\). The square root is why four times as many trials roughly halves sampling uncertainty. For 100 balanced trials, the count standard deviation is five. A difference of three from the expected 50 is ordinary fluctuation. A standard error is not a guarantee that the true value lies in a specified interval. Near zero or one, or for small samples, symmetric normal approximations can be misleading. A likelihood or a binomial interval is preferable. Random uncertainty and systematic error behave differently. Repetition reduces the first, but unequal detection efficiencies can persist indefinitely. If A is detected with efficiency ηA and B with ηB, the observed A fraction among clicks is pηA/[pηA+(1−p)ηB]. Conditioning on detected events changes the population you are describing."}, {"title": "1.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 What the evidence can establish A credible experiment reports a protocol, uncertainties, calibration, and a reason a competing model cannot explain the result equally well. A graph matching a curve is a beginning. It is stronger if the curve's parameters were calibrated independently or if the experiment predicts measurements not used in the fit. Use an assumption ledger throughout this course: write down independence, stable preparation, fair sampling, and the measurement model when they are needed. Later Bell and observer experiments depend on more specific assumptions. Keeping those visible prevents a statement about one model from turning into a claim about every possible explanation. The animation illustrates the convergence of sample frequencies, not the physical origin of randomness. Its statistics are generated by the probability that you choose. It cannot be used to establish the Born rule, certify a random-number generator, or demonstrate a violation of a classical bound."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Choose p=0.5, advance individual events, then run a longer sequence. Predict the scale of fluctuations before increasing N. Reset reproduces the sequence; changing p starts a new dataset. Interactive model · Synthetic observations A detector, one trial at a time A detector, one trial at a time Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Prepare repeatable source Measure two outcomes Count A / B One trial → one recorded outcome On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Probability of A 0.5 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How does a detector click become evidence for a physical claim? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#worked", "text": "A worked example A fair source gives 530 A clicks in 1,000 trials. Is the difference from 500 large? The count standard deviation under the fair model is √250 ≈ 15.8. The excess is 30/15.8 ≈ 1.90 standard deviations. This is not decisive evidence of bias, especially if many datasets were inspected. Check calibration and a prespecified statistical test before making a discovery claim."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 1.1 How many trials reduce the standard error from 0.05 to 0.01 at p=1/2? Hint Use SE=1/(2√N). Worked solution N=2,500. The original error corresponds to N=100, so five times the precision requires 25 times as many trials. Problem 1.2 A fair source is detected with ηA=1 and ηB=1/2. What A fraction appears among clicks? Hint Keep the undetected B events in the denominator until conditioning. Worked solution The fraction is 0.5/(0.5+0.25)=2/3. The source need not be biased; the selected dataset is. Problem 1.3 Would one million simulated clicks validate a detector? Hint Identify where the numbers came from. Worked solution No. They test the simulation and analysis under its assumptions. Detector validation requires measurements of the physical detector, including its response to independently controlled inputs."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward An experimental conclusion joins a preparation, a measurement model, data, and assumptions. More samples do not repair an incorrect measurement model."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "From Clicks to Evidence", "url": "01-evidence.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Feynman Lectures, Volume III, Chapter 1: Quantum Behavior"}, {"title": "2.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 The question and the apparatus Illuminate a metal and collect the electrons it emits. Two knobs have different roles: light frequency changes the energy associated with each photon, while intensity changes the incident flux. A retarding voltage opposes the electrons. The stopping voltage is the point where even the most energetic emitted electrons fail to reach the collector. In a second experiment, excite a dilute gas and resolve its emitted light with a spectrometer. The spectrum contains narrow features associated with transitions between energy levels. In the Franck–Hertz experiment, accelerated electrons lose energy to atoms in discrete excitation events, producing structure in the collected current. These are different apparatuses that constrain the same idea of allowed energy differences. The visual focuses on the photoelectric energy balance. It draws a surface barrier and an emitted-electron energy gauge. It is not a microscopic animation of electron motion within a metal. Real surfaces have work-function variations, contact potentials, finite temperature, and distributions of initial electron energies."}, {"title": "2.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 Build a prediction In the elementary photoelectric model, the maximum kinetic energy is \\(K_{\\max}=hf-\\Phi\\) when hf≥Φ. Here h is Planck's constant, f is frequency, and Φ is the work function. Below threshold this single-photon model predicts no photoemission from the occupied states represented by the model. The stopping potential satisfies eVs=Kmax. Using electron-volts makes the arithmetic simple: an electron crossing one volt gains one electron-volt. For a work function of 2 eV and photon energy of 3 eV, the maximum kinetic energy is 1 eV and the stopping voltage is 1 V. Doubling intensity at fixed frequency increases the event rate in the unsaturated regime; it does not double that maximum energy. For spectroscopy, photon energy is an energy difference: hf=Ei−Ef. A line measures the separation of levels, not their absolute position relative to an arbitrary zero. Repeated Franck–Hertz features require a transport model as well as excitation thresholds; one should not identify every dip with a new atomic level."}, {"title": "2.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 What the evidence can establish A threshold and a linear stopping-voltage relation test a specific energy balance. They do not, by themselves, establish every feature of the quantum electromagnetic field. Some photoelectric observations admit descriptions with a classical field coupled to quantized matter. Chapter 6 introduces photon statistics that more directly distinguish nonclassical light. For a reliable stopping-voltage measurement, subtract dark current, check voltage calibration, and vary frequency across several points. Estimate the slope and intercept with uncertainties instead of extracting a constant from a single point. For spectra, account for instrumental broadening before interpreting linewidth as a lifetime. Our slider changes photon energy while Φ stays at 2 eV. The graph deliberately shows only the permitted maximum energy, including a threshold marker. It does not model multiphoton emission, heating, surface chemistry, or space-charge effects. These limitations explain why the simple prediction is a useful starting model rather than a universal description of illuminated metals."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Sweep photon energy through 2 eV. Locate the threshold, then predict the stopping voltage at 2.5 and 4 eV. Compare the energy gauge with the plotted energy balance. Interactive model · Idealized predictions The photoelectric energy balance The photoelectric energy balance Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Light hf variable frequency Metal surface Φ = 2 eV Collector retarding voltage Kmax = hf − Φ On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Photon energy (eV) 3 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How do spectra and photoelectric measurements reveal energy scales? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#worked", "text": "A worked example Two spectral lines have photon energies 2 eV and 3 eV and share the same lower level. How far apart are their upper levels? Their upper levels differ by 1 eV because the common lower energy cancels. This does not identify the absolute energies of any of the three levels."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 2.1 For Φ=2 eV and hf=3.5 eV, find Kmax and Vs. Hint Use the energy balance and the definition of an electron-volt. Worked solution Kmax=1.5 eV and Vs=1.5 V. Problem 2.2 Does twice the light intensity imply twice the stopping voltage? Hint Separate rate from maximum energy. Worked solution No, in the single-photon unsaturated model the maximum energy is unchanged. More photons can produce more electrons per unit time. Problem 2.3 Why does a spectrum not directly give absolute atomic energy levels? Hint Consider shifting every energy by the same constant. Worked solution Transition frequencies depend on differences. Adding a common energy offset changes no spectral frequency."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Energy-resolved measurements constrain allowed energy exchanges. Separate the photon energy, the count rate, and the assumptions about the material."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Energy Comes in Steps", "url": "02-energy.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Einstein’s photoelectric explanation: Nobel scientific background Franck and Hertz: Nobel scientific background"}, {"title": "3.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 The question and the apparatus Send electrons through two narrow apertures towards a position-sensitive screen. Lower the source rate so simultaneous particles are rare. Each recorded event is localized, yet the accumulated distribution can contain alternating maxima and minima. The central observation concerns the relation between individual events and the distribution of many identically prepared trials. A slit plate sets the geometry; propagation maps transverse momentum to screen position; the detector bins arrivals. Opening only one slit changes the preparation and therefore the expected pattern. Adding a device that records the path changes the joint system. These are different experiments, not merely different stories about an unchanged dataset. The display builds a screen histogram from a model distribution. Moving dots before the screen are symbols for trials, not measured electron trajectories. We draw a broad diffraction envelope and a controllable interference contrast. The model uses a Gaussian envelope for clarity rather than a literal rectangular-aperture diffraction profile."}, {"title": "3.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Build a prediction When two alternatives lead to the same final outcome without a distinguishing record, add their probability amplitudes before squaring: \\(P(x)=|\\psi_L(x)+\\psi_R(x)|^2\\). Expanding gives the two individual intensities plus \\(2\\operatorname{Re}(\\psi_L^*\\psi_R)\\), the interference term. Adding probabilities alone omits that term. For equal amplitudes with a phase difference δ, the intensity is proportional to 1+cosδ. Opposite phases cancel; equal phases reinforce. In a far-field two-slit geometry, δ is approximately 2πdx/(λL), where d is slit separation, L is distance to the screen, and x is transverse position. Adjacent fringes are separated by Δx≈λL/d. For matter waves, λ=h/p. Increasing particle momentum therefore narrows the fringe spacing at fixed geometry. The simulation holds the spacing fixed and varies visibility V in a normalized model proportional to an envelope times [1+V cosδ]. V=0 removes interference but does not remove diffraction."}, {"title": "3.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 What the evidence can establish Single events rule out interpreting the screen solely as a continuous deposited intensity. Their distribution also rules out the particular model that assigns independent slit probabilities and then adds them without interference. It does not rule out every possible trajectory-based theory: such theories can include additional dynamics that reproduce interference. Compare both-slit and single-slit runs under stable source conditions. Account for detector nonuniformity, finite slit widths, coherence length, and vibration. If a path detector is added, check whether the contrast loss is explained by entanglement, random phase shifts, or a mixture of mechanisms. A washed-out pattern alone does not identify the mechanism. The bin counts fluctuate even when the underlying distribution is fixed. The smooth curve is the model expectation and the dots are synthetic samples. Their agreement illustrates sampling, not a new experimental confirmation. Later chapters examine how a path record controls the interference term without requiring a conscious observer."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Keep the visibility at one and watch individual arrivals accumulate. Reset at V=0 and compare the smooth envelope. Explain why a single detection never displays a fringe. Interactive model · Synthetic observations Build an interference pattern Build an interference pattern Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Source Slits Screen Amplitude alternatives On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Visibility 1 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How can separate detection events form an interference pattern? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#worked", "text": "A worked example Electrons with wavelength 0.05 nm pass slits 1 μm apart, with a screen 1 m away. Estimate the fringe spacing. Δx=λL/d=(5×10⁻¹¹ m)(1 m)/(10⁻⁶ m)=5×10⁻⁵ m=50 μm. This estimate assumes far-field geometry and sufficient transverse coherence."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 3.1 Two amplitudes are 1/2 and −1/2. What is their combined probability at this outcome? Hint Add amplitudes first. Worked solution The sum is zero, so the probability is zero. Adding the individual probabilities would incorrectly give 1/2. Problem 3.2 What happens to fringe spacing if momentum doubles? Hint Use λ=h/p. Worked solution The wavelength halves, so Δx halves at fixed slit spacing and screen distance. Problem 3.3 Does losing visible fringes prove a path was observed? Hint Think of other ways to average the phase. Worked solution No. Uncontrolled phase drift can also wash out fringes. Additional measurements are needed to identify the physical mechanism."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Interference is a statement about outcome probabilities under specified alternatives. A diagram of alternatives should not be mistaken for a measured path."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Matter Waves and the Double Slit", "url": "03-matter-waves.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Feynman Lectures, Volume III, Chapter 1: Quantum Behavior"}, {"title": "4.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 The question and the apparatus An inhomogeneous magnetic field couples a particle's magnetic moment to its motion. For a suitable spin-one-half beam, the outgoing wave packets separate into two channels. A slit can select the upper channel, preparing a definite result for a subsequent analyzer along the same axis. Repeating an ideal measurement along that axis then reproduces the selected result. Rotate the second analyzer. A beam prepared as +z splits between the outcomes of an x-oriented analyzer. If we retain the +x output and then measure z, both signs reappear. A classical picture of a fixed little arrow revealed without disturbance cannot explain this sequence without adding a new account of measurement. The historical silver-atom experiment involves atomic structure and translational motion. Our spin model isolates a two-dimensional degree of freedom. The branches in the animation represent separated output channels; their vertical spacing is schematic and is not a calculation of the magnetic force or flight time."}, {"title": "4.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Build a prediction Represent +z and −z by the column vectors (1,0) and (0,1). A spin-up state along an axis at polar angle θ in the xz plane is \\(|+_\\theta\\rangle=\\cos(\\theta/2)|+z\\rangle+\\sin(\\theta/2)|-z\\rangle\\). The probability of +θ after preparing +z is the squared overlap: cos²(θ/2). The half-angle matters. At θ=90°, both outcomes have probability 1/2; at 180°, the +θ output is the −z state. Spin axes are physical directions in space, while the amplitudes inhabit a state space. They should not be identified with the orientation of an ordinary spinning ball. The state after selecting +x is (|+z⟩+|−z⟩)/√2. A final z measurement therefore gives each sign with probability 1/2. Starting with a +z beam, selecting +x transmits half the trials, and then selecting +z transmits half of those. The net transmission is 1/4. Conditional and unconditional probabilities answer different questions."}, {"title": "4.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 What the evidence can establish Sequential experiments test both outcome probabilities and the state changes associated with measurement. A detector that reports +x and then re-prepares +z would have the same immediate reporting statistics as an ideal x measurement but different later statistics. The observable alone does not specify the entire measurement procedure. In a physical apparatus, imperfect separation, lost particles, and unwanted spin rotations affect the ideal sequence. Normalize counts to the relevant input population. If you retain one output, label it as postselection rather than implying that every input particle survives. Selection changes which trials reach the next stage. The simulation starts with +z and rotates a single analyzer, displaying its two probabilities. Use the worked sequence to extend it to a chain. It illustrates the spin-one-half rule but does not claim that the measured particle had a definite value for every unperformed measurement."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Rotate the analyzer from 0° through 90° to 180°. Predict the two branch weights before moving the slider. Relate the changing weights to cos²(θ/2), not cos²θ. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Rotate the analyzer Rotate the analyzer Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Prepare +z Magnet angle θ + axis − axis Spatial outputs encode measurement results. On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Analyzer angle (degrees) 90 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Why does inserting a different measurement change later outcomes? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#worked", "text": "A worked example From 800 prepared +z particles, how many ideally survive a +x filter followed by a +z filter? The first selection retains 800×1/2=400 on average. The second retains 400×1/2=200. Without the intermediate x filter, the ideal +z filter retains all 800."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 4.1 What is P(+θ) at θ=60° for +z input? Hint Use the half-angle. Worked solution cos²30°=3/4. Problem 4.2 After selecting −x, what is P(+z)? Hint Write the −x state in the z basis. Worked solution The state is (|+z⟩−|−z⟩)/√2, so P(+z)=1/2. The minus sign affects x interference, not these z probabilities. Problem 4.3 Why must the survival fraction be reported with the final outcome ratio? Hint Compare the original beam with the selected beam. Worked solution The selected ensemble may have a simple outcome ratio while most input particles were discarded. Without the survival fraction, the protocol cannot be reconstructed."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward A measurement is an operation with outcomes and a conditional state change. Its position in a sequence affects what happens next."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Stern–Gerlach and Sequential Measurements", "url": "04-stern-gerlach.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Feynman Lectures, Volume III, Chapter 6: Spin One-Half"}, {"title": "5.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 The question and the apparatus Prepare horizontally polarized light and place a vertical analyzer in its path. An ideal analyzer transmits none. Now insert a third polarizer between them, at an angle θ from horizontal. Some light reaches the output. The extra filter changes the state reaching the final analyzer; it is not simply removing members of an unchanged collection. Polarization is a useful bridge between classical waves and quantum states. Classical electric-field components obey the same two-component amplitude mathematics as a single photon's polarization. An intensity demonstration alone is therefore not evidence that the light consists of single photons. The photon version interprets normalized transmitted intensity as a transmission probability for identically prepared photons. We assume ideal linear polarizers, a fixed propagation direction, and a detector that counts all transmitted light equally. Real polarizers have finite extinction ratios and insertion losses. Never point an unattenuated beam or a laser at an eye; the browser activity needs no physical optical equipment."}, {"title": "5.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Build a prediction Choose |H⟩=(1,0) and |V⟩=(0,1). The transmitted state of an analyzer at θ is |θ⟩=cosθ|H⟩+sinθ|V⟩. Its overlap with H is cosθ, giving Malus's law P=cos²θ. Unlike the spin-axis formula in Chapter 4, the angle here directly describes an optical polarization direction. After transmission through the middle filter, the state is |θ⟩. Its probability of passing the vertical filter is sin²θ. Multiplying the conditional probabilities gives \\(P_{H\\to\\theta\\to V}=\\cos^2\\theta\\sin^2\\theta=\\tfrac14\\sin^2(2\\theta)\\). The maximum is 1/4 at θ=45°. The order is essential. Placing the angled filter after the crossed H and V pair cannot restore light already absorbed. Matrix multiplication captures the order: the output amplitude is PV Pθ|H⟩, where each P is a projection onto the transmitted state. Generally these projections do not commute."}, {"title": "5.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 What the evidence can establish The experiment shows why a filter is both a selection and a preparation. It also gives an accessible example of measurement order. It does not establish consciousness-induced collapse or distinguish quantum theory from classical polarization optics. Stronger claims require additional observables, such as the counting statistics of Chapter 6. Measure the input intensity after the preparation filter, not before it, if you want to compare directly with our probability. For initially unpolarized light, the first ideal polarizer transmits half the incident intensity, making the maximum transmission through all three filters 1/8 of the original. The visual rotates the middle transmission axis and shows the probability after each filter. The line orientation is a polarization symbol, not a literal drawing of a photon's spatial shape. The animation follows the order of operations while the graph displays the corresponding prediction."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Find the middle-filter angle giving maximum output. Then move it to 0° and 90° and explain the zero in each case. Follow both the intermediate and final transmission. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Three polarizers Three polarizers Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. H preparation θ analyzer V analyzer On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Middle angle (degrees) 45 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Why can adding a polarizer increase transmission through crossed filters? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#worked", "text": "A worked example Start with 1,000 H-polarized trials and set the middle filter to 30°. What is the mean final count? The middle filter transmits 3/4. The final vertical filter transmits 1/4 of those, so the mean is 1,000×3/16=187.5. An actual count is an integer and fluctuates around that mean."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 5.1 What is the maximum transmission relative to an H-prepared input? Hint Maximize sin²(2θ). Worked solution 1/4, reached at 45° within the 0°–90° range. Problem 5.2 Does a classical laser demonstration prove photon quantization? Hint Ask which classical wave prediction differs. Worked solution No. Classical polarization optics predicts the same normalized intensities here. Problem 5.3 Why does putting the angled filter after V fail? Hint Apply the crossed projections first. Worked solution PV|H⟩=0. Once the ideal crossed pair transmits no amplitude, a later passive filter has no transmitted field to act on."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Track the state after each operation. A matching amplitude formula does not make every optical demonstration uniquely quantum."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Polarization and Three Filters", "url": "05-polarization.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Feynman Lectures, Volume III, Chapter 11: More Two-State Systems"}, {"title": "6.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 The question and the apparatus Direct a light source onto a balanced beam splitter and place a detector at each output. Record both the single-detector counts and pairs of detections separated by a delay τ. A coincidence histogram asks how likely another detection is near a detection already recorded. It measures correlations, not just average brightness. A single-photon input ideally cannot trigger both outputs in the same trial. A weak laser is different: attenuation reduces its average photon number but leaves a nonzero multiphoton probability. A dim source is therefore not automatically a single-photon source. In heralded experiments, a partner detection identifies the trials in which a photon is expected. The animation compares idealized antibunched, coherent, and bunched light. It shows a normalized correlation curve rather than laboratory time tags. Detector dead time, timing jitter, dark counts, and accidental coincidences are omitted from the curve but must be included when analysing an instrument."}, {"title": "6.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Build a prediction The normalized second-order correlation can be written \\(g^{(2)}(0)=\\langle n(n-1)\\rangle/\\langle n\\rangle^2\\) for a single mode with nonzero mean. For a one-photon number state, n(n−1)=0 and g⁽²⁾(0)=0. For ideal coherent light the value is one. A single-mode thermal field gives two. The browser uses representative delay profiles: 1−exp(−|τ|/τc) for antibunching, one for coherent light, and 1+exp(−|τ|/τc) for bunching. These expose the dip or peak but are not universal source models. Real resonance fluorescence can have oscillations, and multimode thermal light need not reach two. Why is a value below one interesting? A classical fluctuating nonnegative intensity satisfies ⟨I²⟩≥⟨I⟩². Under the corresponding photodetection model this implies g⁽²⁾(0)≥1. Sub-Poissonian or antibunched statistics violate that classical intensity description. The assumptions about detection and normalization remain part of the inference."}, {"title": "6.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 What the evidence can establish Use two detectors because one detector's dead time can itself produce an apparent absence of nearby clicks. Estimate accidentals from appropriate displaced windows, report the coincidence-bin width, and characterize background. A very narrow uncorrected dip is not persuasive if the instrument suppresses close events by construction. Antibunching is evidence for nonclassical light; it does not guarantee purity of polarization, perfect indistinguishability, or high collection efficiency. Those are different properties requiring different measurements. Chapter 16 uses two-photon interference to examine mode overlap. The display supplies the curves by definition, so it cannot certify a source. Its purpose is to connect different questions—brightness, photon number, and timing correlations—to different measurements. A published source characterization should supply raw or suitably processed coincidence data and explain the corrections."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Switch among the three source models. Compare the zero-delay value with the long-delay baseline. Explain why lowering brightness alone does not change a coherent source into an antibunched one. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Correlations between clicks Correlations between clicks Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Light source choose statistics Detector A Detector B Compare detection times: delay τ On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Delay τ / τc 0 Configuration Antibunched Coherent Thermal, one mode Play Step Reset Read the values as a table What makes a source of light nonclassical? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#worked", "text": "A worked example A distribution contains one photon with probability 0.2 and vacuum otherwise. Calculate g⁽²⁾(0). The mean photon number is 0.2. Since n is always zero or one, ⟨n(n−1)⟩=0 and g⁽²⁾(0)=0. Low delivery probability can coexist with ideal antibunching; this statistic does not measure efficiency."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 6.1 What is g⁽²⁾(0) for exactly two photons? Hint Compute the numerator and denominator separately. Worked solution 2×1/2²=1/2. A value below one need not mean exactly one photon. Problem 6.2 Why use a second detector? Hint Consider the response immediately after one detector fires. Worked solution A single detector may be unable to fire again during its dead time. Two independent outputs help distinguish source correlations from that artifact. Problem 6.3 Can perfect antibunching establish polarization purity? Hint Identify which degree of freedom was measured. Worked solution No. Photon-number statistics do not determine polarization. Polarization tomography is a separate measurement."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Brightness and nonclassicality are different measurements. Correlation claims need an explicit detection and normalization model."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Single Photons and Coincidences", "url": "06-photon-counting.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Grangier, Roger and Aspect: Experimental evidence for a photon anticorrelation effect"}, {"title": "7.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 The question and the apparatus A Mach–Zehnder interferometer splits a beam into two paths, redirects them with mirrors, and recombines them at a second beam splitter. A phase plate changes the relative phase. With suitable alignment, all ideal single-photon trials can arrive at one output even though either path would separately feed both outputs. Both paths must describe indistinguishable alternatives at recombination. Different polarization, frequency, arrival time, or an attached path record can reduce visibility. The apparatus is therefore a controlled comparison of alternatives, not merely a way of splitting an intensity in half twice. The drawing represents the source, two beam splitters, a phase element, and two detectors. Glowing path segments show amplitude alternatives in the model. They should not be read as observations of a photon travelling along two measured trajectories."}, {"title": "7.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 Build a prediction Use a real balanced beam splitter represented by the Hadamard matrix H. This phase convention is mathematically convenient; an optical implementation may distribute reflection phases differently. Starting in |0⟩, the first splitter prepares (|0⟩+|1⟩)/√2. A phase element produces (|0⟩+e iφ |1⟩)/√2. The second H gives output amplitudes (1+e iφ )/2 and (1−e iφ )/2. Thus \\(P_0=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\) and \\(P_1=(1-\\cos\\phi)/2\\). They sum to one. At φ=0, output 1 is dark; at φ=π, output 0 is dark. At φ=π/2 both outputs have probability 1/2. If an uncontrolled process reduces the coherence by a real factor V, the same calculation gives P0=(1+V cosφ)/2. The contrast of the observed fringe is (Pmax−Pmin)/(Pmax+Pmin). A single phase setting cannot distinguish a phase error from reduced contrast; scan the phase."}, {"title": "7.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 What the evidence can establish Classical coherent waves reproduce the intensity fringes. The single-photon version combines interference with indivisible detections, but the fringe shape alone is not a universal witness of quantization. The interpretation depends on how the source was characterized and what was recorded. To measure visibility, stabilize path lengths and collect counts across a full phase period. Fit detector offsets and efficiency differences instead of automatically interpreting every imbalance as phase. A source drift during a sequential scan can mimic a distorted fringe; interleaving phase settings is one possible control. This simple experiment will be reused in delayed choice, quantum erasure, and interaction-free measurement. Each modifies a different physical operation. Keeping the same amplitude convention makes those modifications easy to calculate without introducing stories about what the photon must have known."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Sweep φ through a full turn. Pause at 0°, 90°, and 180° and predict the detector probabilities. Watch the amplitude markers meet at recombination rather than interpreting them as particle tracks. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Recombine the alternatives Recombine the alternatives Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Input BS1 BS2 D0 D1 Phase φ On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Phase (degrees) 0 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How can two individually random beam splitters give a certain output? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#worked", "text": "A worked example What phase gives P0=3/4 in the ideal interferometer? Solve (1+cosφ)/2=3/4, giving cosφ=1/2. Within one full turn the solutions are φ=60° and 300°. One probability alone therefore does not fix the phase uniquely."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 7.1 What happens if the second beam splitter is removed? Hint Measure the path-basis populations before H. Worked solution The two detectors each receive probability 1/2, independent of φ. The relative phase is not visible in path counts. Problem 7.2 For V=0.6, find the maximum and minimum P0. Hint Set cosφ to ±1. Worked solution The extrema are 0.8 and 0.2, giving visibility (0.8−0.2)/(0.8+0.2)=0.6. Problem 7.3 Why must both output probabilities sum to one? Hint Use a lossless two-mode model. Worked solution Unitary evolution preserves the norm. If measured probabilities do not sum to one relative to emitted trials, include loss or additional outcomes."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward A relative phase becomes a population difference only after a suitable recombination. State the beam-splitter convention and keep it consistent."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Mach–Zehnder: Adding Alternatives", "url": "07-interferometer.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Feynman Lectures, Volume III, Chapter 3: Probability Amplitudes"}, {"title": "8.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 The question and the apparatus Imagine mounting part of a double-slit apparatus on a support sensitive enough to register momentum recoil. The recoil might distinguish which path a particle took. Could one retain the usual interference pattern while also reading a perfect path record? This thought experiment asks whether the measuring apparatus can be excluded from the quantum description. Treat the movable support as a quantum system. The two path alternatives leave it in states |dL⟩ and |dR⟩. If these states are identical, the support stores no path information. If they are orthogonal, a suitable measurement can distinguish them perfectly. The support's mass alone does not decide this; the distinguishability of its possible states does. The visual draws two Gaussian pointer amplitudes separated in a dimensionless readout coordinate. Their overlap is the important variable. The coordinate is a toy model for an apparatus record, not a literal mechanical trajectory of Einstein's original slit arrangement."}, {"title": "8.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 Build a prediction After the interaction, the joint state has the form (|L⟩|dL⟩+|R⟩|dR⟩)/√2. Ignoring the detector means tracing it out. The off-diagonal path term is multiplied by γ=⟨dR|dL⟩. For equal path weights and otherwise ideal alignment, the visibility is V=|γ|. For real Gaussian amplitudes of equal width σ whose probability distributions have variance σ² and whose centres are separated by Δ, the overlap is \\(\\gamma=\\exp[-\\Delta^2/(8\\sigma^2)]\\). Our control is Δ/σ. Moving the pointer distributions apart reduces the cross term smoothly; there is no special threshold at which a human decides to look. For two equally likely pure detector states, optimal distinguishability is \\(D=\\sqrt{1-|\\gamma|^2}\\). In this restricted model D²+V²=1. More general imperfections give inequalities and require careful definitions. Do not apply this equality indiscriminately to every noisy interferometer."}, {"title": "8.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 What the evidence can establish The calculation locates complementarity in correlations with the apparatus. A recorded recoil can remove local interference even before anyone reads the record. An uncontrolled random phase can also reduce visibility, so observing low contrast alone does not prove that an accessible record exists. One can try to measure the detector in a different basis and sort the particle events conditionally. That leads to quantum erasure in Chapter 10. It changes which joint correlations are displayed; it does not restore an unconditional pattern merely by refusing to read a classical record. As a thought experiment, the setup is most useful when its assumptions are explicit: coherent preparation, a controlled apparatus interaction, known detector states, and an ideal recombiner. A real implementation needs an independently characterized coupling and detector response. The two-Gaussian visualization demonstrates the mathematics of distinguishability, not a historical reconstruction."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Separate the two pointer distributions. Compare overlap, visibility, and optimal distinguishability. Find a separation where neither the path information nor the interference is perfect. Interactive model · Idealized predictions A record becomes distinguishable A record becomes distinguishable Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Two paths L / R Movable record pointer coordinate Readout Pointer states overlap or separate Greater distinguishability → less local interference On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Separation Δ / σ 2 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Can a movable slit record a path without changing interference? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#worked", "text": "A worked example At Δ=2σ, calculate the visibility and distinguishability. V=e⁻¹ᐟ²≈0.607. Then D=√(1−e⁻¹)≈0.795. Their squared values sum to one within rounding. Neither perfect path knowledge nor perfect visibility is available."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 8.1 What is the visibility when the two detector states coincide? Hint Their inner product is one. Worked solution V=1 and D=0 in the ideal balanced pure-state model. Problem 8.2 Does leaving a perfect path detector unread restore fringes? Hint Trace over its orthogonal states. Worked solution No. Orthogonal path records make the local cross term zero whether or not a person reads them. Problem 8.3 Why is D²+V²=1 not a universal laboratory calibration? Hint List the assumptions of the derivation. Worked solution It assumes balanced paths, pure detector states, optimal discrimination, and no other loss of coherence. Mixed states and technical imperfections require a more general treatment."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Include the measuring apparatus in the state. The overlap of possible records controls interference in the reduced system."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Einstein’s Recoiling Slit", "url": "08-recoiling-slit.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Englert: Fringe visibility and which-way information"}, {"title": "9.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 The question and the apparatus Start with the Mach–Zehnder interferometer. After the input has passed the first beam splitter, choose whether to insert the second. With it present, the paths recombine and the output depends on phase. With it absent, the output ports resolve the two path modes. Wheeler's question challenges the assumption that a photon must first decide to behave as a wave or a particle. The sequence matters: preparation, first splitter, phase accumulation, setting choice, final detection. A delayed choice is still an ordinary choice of a later measurement operation. It does not replace an already recorded detection with another result. A convincing experimental implementation specifies the timing and separation of the setting choice and the photon's passage. The display lets you switch between open and closed configurations and step through that sequence. Its moving indicator is a timing guide. It is not a hidden history showing where a photon really travelled between measurements."}, {"title": "9.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 Build a prediction Immediately before the possible second splitter, write |ψ⟩=(|0⟩+e iφ |1⟩)/√2. For the open apparatus, the probabilities are the path populations, both 1/2. For the closed apparatus, applying H gives P0=(1+cosφ)/2 and P1=(1−cosφ)/2. The earlier state need not be recalculated backwards in time. These are two different observables on the same incoming state. A state can give equal probabilities in one basis and a certain result in another. Calling the first result particle-like and the second wave-like is a description of the measurement statistics, not a dynamical law forcing an earlier identity switch. The animation uses a balanced, lossless model. At φ=0, closing the interferometer changes the prediction from two equal output probabilities to one certain output. Choosing later changes which transformation is physically applied before detection. It leaves any earlier saved records unchanged."}, {"title": "9.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 What the evidence can establish Laboratory delayed-choice experiments implement the relevant timing and compare the two configurations. They test interference and path-sensitive measurements under a late setting selection. They do not establish that the future sends a message into the past. The published apparatus must be examined to understand what information could propagate between events. An interpretation can offer a different account of unobserved history while agreeing on these probabilities. This course distinguishes that interpretive freedom from the experimentally checkable prediction. A surprising narrative is not an extra observable. When using the display, keep the detection setting and phase fixed for a batch of synthetic trials. Comparing batches demonstrates the different probability models. It does not reproduce the spacetime arrangement, random setting generator, source characterization, or detection calibration of a physical delayed-choice experiment."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Set φ=0 and compare the open and closed arrangements. Step through the timing guide and identify the operation that converts phase into an output imbalance. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Choose the final measurement Choose the final measurement Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Input BS1 BS2 D0 D1 Phase φ Choice: keep / remove BS2 On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Phase (degrees) 0 Configuration Closed interferometer Open interferometer Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Does choosing the measurement late change what happened earlier? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#worked", "text": "A worked example At φ=90°, open and closed arrangements both give equal detector counts. Are the arrangements equivalent? No. Their predictions coincide at that particular phase. At φ=0 the open device gives 1/2,1/2 and the closed device gives 1,0. Scan a parameter before declaring two models experimentally equivalent."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 9.1 Which matrix is applied when the second splitter is absent? Hint Use the identity in the path basis. Worked solution The identity leaves the path populations unchanged, so both probabilities remain 1/2. Problem 9.2 Does the output distribution tell us an earlier path with certainty in the closed device? Hint Identify the measured basis. Worked solution No. The closed device measures recombined alternatives. Its phase-sensitive output is not a record of one earlier slit or arm. Problem 9.3 What extra information is required to assess a delayed-choice laboratory claim? Hint Think beyond a histogram. Worked solution The event timing, setting-choice process, relevant distances, source properties, and detector response are needed to understand what causal influences the arrangement excludes."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward A late measurement choice changes the operation before detection. The quantum prediction requires no rewriting of earlier records."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Wheeler’s Delayed Choice", "url": "09-delayed-choice.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Jacques et al.: Experimental realization of Wheeler’s delayed-choice experiment"}, {"title": "10.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 The question and the apparatus Correlate the two paths with orthogonal marker states, for example horizontal and vertical polarization of a partner system. Looking only at the signal gives no interference. Instead of reading the marker in the path-identifying basis, measure it in a superposition basis and sort signal detections by the marker outcome. The sorted subsets can exhibit complementary interference patterns. The full dataset does not. This distinction between joint data and a selected subset is the essential experimental idea. Discarding a file containing path labels is not the same physical operation as measuring a coherent marker in a new basis. The visual shows three distributions together: all signal events, events paired with marker +, and events paired with marker −. Each conditional curve is separately normalized. The weighted sum, not the unweighted visual height of a selected subset, predicts what the signal observer sees alone."}, {"title": "10.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Build a prediction Take the ideal state (|L⟩|H⟩+e iφ |R⟩|V⟩)/√2. In the marker basis |±⟩=(|H⟩±|V⟩)/√2, the conditional signal states are (|L⟩±e iφ |R⟩)/√2. The sign changes the interference term, shifting maxima of one subset to minima of the other. After recombination, \\(P(0\\mid +)=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\) and \\(P(0\\mid -)=(1-\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Each marker sign occurs with probability 1/2. Therefore P(0)=ΣmP(m)P(0|m)=1/2 for every φ. At a screen, the same cancellation occurs between complementary fringes within an envelope. The ordering of measurements on separate subsystems does not change these ideal joint probabilities, because the local measurement operators commute. Sorting a previously collected dataset later can reveal a correlation without changing any earlier event. The sorting requires the marker records to be communicated."}, {"title": "10.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 What the evidence can establish An eraser experiment supports predictions about correlations and conditioning. It does not enable faster-than-light communication: a signal observer without the marker labels sees the same marginal distribution. Nor does a delayed marker measurement alter the past screen image. The later operation determines which conditional description is useful. For real coincidence experiments, the time window and pairing rule matter. Accidental pairs dilute visibility; selecting windows after seeing the desired pattern can bias a result. Report both sorted subsets, their rates, and the unsorted data. Showing only one attractive fringe leaves out the cancellation that explains the experiment. Our display assumes an ideal maximally entangled marker and equal subset weights. Imperfect entanglement, background, and unequal efficiencies need a larger model. The linked experiment is a physical implementation; the browser curves are an independently specified teaching model, not its data."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Change the phase and compare the two conditional curves. Check that their equal-weight average stays flat. Explain what information a distant signal observer would need to reproduce either subset. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Sort the same signal records Sort the same signal records Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Input BS1 BS2 D0 D1 Phase φ Path marker H / V → ± On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Phase (degrees) 0 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Where are the fringes hiding when path information is available? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#worked", "text": "A worked example At φ=0, what are P(0,+), P(0,−), and P(0)? P(0,+)=P(+)P(0|+)=1/2. P(0,−)=0. Their sum is P(0)=1/2. A certain conditional outcome does not mean a certain unconditional outcome."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 10.1 Why do opposite fringes not constitute a signal at the unsorted detector? Hint Average with the marker probabilities. Worked solution The cosine terms cancel in the equal-weight sum. The local marginal is independent of the eraser outcome. Problem 10.2 Is deleting a classical path log a quantum eraser? Hint Compare a classical record with a controllable coherent marker. Worked solution No. Deletion does not undo the correlations with uncontrolled degrees of freedom or implement a superposition-basis measurement. Problem 10.3 What must be reported alongside a selected coincidence fringe? Hint Consider normalization and selection. Worked solution The opposite subset, subset frequencies, unsorted counts, pairing rule, and uncertainty are needed to assess the correlation and rule out selection artifacts."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Conditional fringes can coexist with an unmodulated marginal. Always write the conditioning event next to the probability."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Quantum Erasure and Conditional Fringes", "url": "10-quantum-eraser.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Kim et al.: A delayed-choice quantum eraser"}, {"title": "11.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#section-11-1", "text": "11.1 The question and the apparatus Balance an interferometer so output D1 is dark when both arms are open. Place an ideal absorber in one arm. In the original thought experiment the absorber is a sensitive bomb, but an opaque object suffices for the model. A photon hitting it is absorbed; a photon reaching the previously dark output can reveal its presence without an absorption in that successful trial. The protocol has three outcomes: absorption, a bright-port detection that is inconclusive, and a dark-port detection that certifies the obstruction under ideal assumptions. Keeping all three is essential. The phrase interaction-free does not mean that every attempt avoids the object or that the entire arrangement has no physical interaction Hamiltonian. Our drawing uses an absorber symbol and probability bars. It is entirely a browser model. The question concerns the logic of alternatives and null outcomes, not practical handling of dangerous devices."}, {"title": "11.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#section-11-2", "text": "11.2 Build a prediction The first balanced splitter sends probability 1/2 towards the absorber. That branch is lost. Conditional on survival, only the other path remains; the second splitter sends half of the surviving probability to each detector. Thus \\(P_{\\rm absorb}=1/2\\), \\(P_{D0}=1/4\\), and \\(P_{D1}=1/4\\). With no object, both amplitudes reach the recombiner and D1 has probability zero. Therefore a D1 click distinguishes an obstruction from the empty ideal interferometer. A D0 click occurs in both cases and is inconclusive. The absence of interference is physically relevant even though the successful detector event did not deposit energy in the absorber. Among nonabsorbed trials, half are successful certifications. Among all incident trials, only one quarter are. Another convention counts success divided by success plus absorption, giving 1/3 here. Always state the denominator before quoting an efficiency. Repeated weak interrogations can change the tradeoff but require a different protocol."}, {"title": "11.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#section-11-3", "text": "11.3 What the evidence can establish An imperfectly balanced empty interferometer already has dark-port counts. A dark click then supplies evidence with a false-positive probability rather than logical certainty. Detector dark counts and partial transparency likewise change the inference. Characterize the empty-device baseline before introducing the object. This protocol demonstrates that an unused alternative can affect observable probabilities. It does not establish the absence of every conceivable interaction in every interpretation. The operational claim is precise: in a successful ideal dark-port trial, an object is inferred without the modeled absorption event. The simulator toggles an opaque absorber and compares the normalized probabilities of all outcomes. It does not simulate high-efficiency quantum-Zeno variants. Those extensions are a useful project only after the single-pass accounting is understood."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Compare the empty and obstructed devices. Follow all three outcome probabilities. Explain why a dark-port event is informative but a bright-port event is not decisive. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Keep every outcome in view Keep every outcome in view Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Input BS1 BS2 D0 D1 Phase φ Absorber one arm On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Protocol stage 0 Configuration Opaque object present Object absent Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Can an object be detected in a trial in which it absorbs nothing? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#worked", "text": "A worked example For 400 incident photons with an ideal opaque object, what mean counts should be reported? 200 absorptions, 100 bright-port events, and 100 dark-port certifications. Reporting only the 200 surviving events would hide half the cost of the protocol."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 11.1 What is the unconditional certification probability? Hint Use all incoming trials. Worked solution 1/4 for the balanced single-pass protocol. Problem 11.2 Can a dark click prove an object is present if the empty baseline is nonzero? Hint Compare the two likelihoods. Worked solution It is evidence rather than certainty. A posterior probability also depends on the prior probability of an object and the calibrated false-positive rate. Problem 11.3 Why does an opaque object not simply halve the original bright output? Hint Condition on the surviving path before recombination. Worked solution With one path removed, the cancellation at the dark port is absent. The surviving half is split between both outputs."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward A successful event and a successful protocol are different accounting questions. Include absorbed and inconclusive trials."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "The Elitzur–Vaidman Bomb Tester", "url": "11-interaction-free.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Elitzur and Vaidman: Quantum mechanical interaction-free measurements"}, {"title": "12.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#section-12-1", "text": "12.1 The question and the apparatus Consider a one-dimensional particle of energy E incident on a finite rectangular potential barrier of height V0>E and width a. Classical motion with that energy cannot enter and cross the forbidden region. Quantum scattering instead matches a wavefunction and its derivative across both interfaces, leaving a nonzero transmitted amplitude. In scanning tunnelling microscopy, a conducting tip is brought close to a surface and a tunnelling current is measured under an applied bias. The current depends strongly on separation, allowing sensitive imaging. A real image also depends on electronic states, voltage, and tip structure; it is not a simple photograph of hard atomic spheres. The visual shows an ideal barrier and a stationary amplitude envelope. It does not depict a particle borrowing energy or accelerating through a wall. The transmitted component has the same energy as the incident component in this time-independent elastic model."}, {"title": "12.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#section-12-2", "text": "12.2 Build a prediction Inside the barrier, the time-independent Schrödinger equation gives exponential solutions with \\(\\kappa=\\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}/\\hbar\\). For an opaque barrier, the leading width dependence is T∝e −2κa . This approximation describes the sensitivity to width but omits a prefactor and should not be used at a=0. For equal potentials on both sides, exact matching gives \\(T=[1+V_0^2\\sinh^2(\\kappa a)/(4E(V_0-E))]^{-1}\\). The browser fixes E/V0=1/2, making T=sech²(κa). This correctly tends to one as the width goes to zero. Reflection has probability R=1−T for this lossless model. An evanescent amplitude is not a negative probability, and a nonzero tail is not by itself a particle trajectory. Transmission is determined by probability current through the full matched solution. The drawn envelope is schematic; the displayed transmission uses the exact restricted formula above."}, {"title": "12.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#section-12-3", "text": "12.3 What the evidence can establish A measured exponential dependence on distance supports tunnelling models, but extracting a barrier height requires assumptions about effective mass, geometry, and available electronic states. Temperature-dependent activation and leakage paths can complicate an interpretation. Compare alternative mechanisms across more than one control parameter. The experiment does not violate energy conservation: a static barrier exchanges no net energy with the particle. Time-dependent barriers and inelastic scattering are different problems. Similarly, a stationary transmission coefficient is not a direct measurement of a unique time spent under the barrier. This lesson connects a textbook scattering calculation to a measurement principle. The interactive model gives a transparent control over barrier width and records predicted transmission. It should not be used to calculate a real microscope current without a material and electrode model."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Increase the dimensionless barrier width κa. Compare T and R and note the rapid suppression. Return to zero width to check the exact formula against its thin-barrier limit. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Transmission through a barrier Transmission through a barrier Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. V₀ E = V₀/2 Width a Incoming + reflected Transmitted On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Width κa 1 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How can transmission occur when the particle energy is below a barrier? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#worked", "text": "A worked example For κa=1 at E/V0=1/2, estimate transmission. cosh(1)≈1.543, so T=1/cosh²(1)≈0.420 and R≈0.580. The crude exponential alone, e⁻²≈0.135, misses the prefactor and is not accurate in this regime."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 12.1 What is T at zero width? Hint Take sinh(0)=0 in the exact expression. Worked solution T=1. No finite barrier remains. Problem 12.2 Does a transmitted particle lose energy in this model? Hint The potential is time independent and equal on both sides. Worked solution No. Elastic transmission preserves its energy; the probability changes, not the outgoing energy. Problem 12.3 What is the approximate change in T when a thick barrier grows by 1/κ? Hint Use the exponential dependence only in its valid regime. Worked solution T is multiplied by approximately e⁻²≈0.135, with other parameters fixed."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Tunnelling is a boundary-matching prediction with measurable transmission. It does not require temporary violations of energy conservation."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Tunnelling Through a Barrier", "url": "12-tunnelling.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Binnig and Rohrer: Scanning tunnelling microscopy, Nobel lecture"}, {"title": "13.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#section-13-1", "text": "13.1 The question and the apparatus Prepare an atom, ion, or effective qubit in its ground state. Apply an oscillating field near its transition frequency for a controlled duration, then measure the excited-state population. Repeating for different durations produces oscillations rather than a monotonic accumulation of excitation. The drive controls an amplitude, not merely an excitation rate. Initialization, drive calibration, and readout are separate operations. A device can have excellent coherent control and poor measurement fidelity, or the reverse. Measure the ground and excited readout responses independently before attributing a small oscillation contrast entirely to the drive. Our display uses a Bloch sphere: its north pole is |0⟩, its south pole |1⟩, and equatorial points are equal-population superpositions with different phases. The arrow represents a state, not the direction of a classical object's spin in ordinary space. Its rotation is a compact picture of the amplitude calculation."}, {"title": "13.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#section-13-2", "text": "13.2 Build a prediction In a resonant rotating-frame approximation, the Hamiltonian is H=ℏΩX/2. Evolving |0⟩ for time t gives \\(|\\psi(t)\\rangle=\\cos(\\Omega t/2)|0\\rangle-i\\sin(\\Omega t/2)|1\\rangle\\). Squaring the second amplitude gives P1=sin²(Ωt/2). The pulse area θ=Ωt is the angle used in the animation. A π pulse ideally transfers the population to |1⟩. A π/2 pulse creates equal populations, but its relative phase depends on the drive phase. This is why measuring only population after one pulse cannot completely characterize the prepared state. With detuning Δ and a constant drive, the ideal excitation is [Ω²/(Ω²+Δ²)]sin²(√(Ω²+Δ²)t/2). Detuning changes both the amplitude and frequency. The explorer deliberately uses resonance; it does not silently interpret a detuned or decaying curve as the same model."}, {"title": "13.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#section-13-3", "text": "13.3 What the evidence can establish Fit a duration scan with uncertainties, check the readout calibration, and repeat at several drive powers. A coherent-drive model predicts a systematic dependence of Ω on the field amplitude. A fit alone does not establish that leakage to higher levels or pulse distortion is negligible. The rotating-wave and two-level approximations have a range of validity. Strong drives or nearby transitions can invalidate them. Calibration on isolated pulses may also fail in a longer sequence because of drift, heating, or interaction with other systems. The animation traces a coherent rotation and plots its expected population. It does not estimate fidelity from a single oscillation. Process characterization and benchmarking require additional preparations and measurements; the course later introduces state tomography as a first step."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Advance the pulse area by small steps. Locate π/2, π, and 2π. Compare the arrow with the excited-state probability and explain why equal populations are not a unique state. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Rotate a state with a pulse Rotate a state with a pulse Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. |0⟩ |1⟩ x y Prepare |0⟩ calibrate readout Controlled evolution pulse / delay Measure populations repeat on fresh systems On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Pulse area (degrees) 0 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How does a pulse prepare a chosen quantum state? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#worked", "text": "A worked example A resonant Rabi frequency Ω/2π is 5 MHz. How long is a π pulse? tπ=π/Ω=1/(2×5 MHz)=100 ns. The π/2 pulse takes 50 ns under the same constant drive."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 13.1 What is P1 after a 2π pulse? Hint Evaluate sin²π. Worked solution Zero. The state returns to the same physical ray, with an overall minus sign. Problem 13.2 Can P1=1/2 certify a coherent superposition? Hint Compare a pure state with a balanced mixture. Worked solution No. Both can give half population. A measurement in another basis is needed to test coherence. Problem 13.3 If the pulse area is π+ε, what is the leading population error? Hint Expand cos²(ε/2). Worked solution P1≈1−ε²/4, so the population error is approximately ε²/4 for small ε."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Coherent control rotates amplitudes. Population scans calibrate part of that control but do not fully specify the prepared state."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Rabi Oscillations and Coherent Control", "url": "13-rabi.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Feynman Lectures, Volume III, Chapter 9: The Ammonia Maser"}, {"title": "14.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#section-14-1", "text": "14.1 The question and the apparatus Apply a π/2 pulse, wait for time t, then apply a second π/2 pulse and measure. During the wait, a frequency difference between the system and the reference drive accumulates a phase. The second pulse converts that phase into a population difference. This is Ramsey interferometry, with time playing the role of the separation between interferometer arms. Repeated runs can lose contrast because individual systems acquire different phases or because uncontrolled environment interactions carry away coherence. A spin-echo pulse halfway through the wait can refocus static frequency offsets. It cannot generally reverse energy relaxation or arbitrary fluctuating noise. The visual compares a damped Ramsey fringe with an idealized echo envelope. It fixes a phenomenological dephasing time and a chosen oscillation period. The echo comparison represents perfectly refocusable static spread without other decoherence; it is an upper-limit toy model, not a promise of perfect recovery in a laboratory."}, {"title": "14.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#section-14-2", "text": "14.2 Build a prediction For a suitable final-pulse phase convention, P0(t)=[1+V(t)cos(Δt)]/2. An exponential model uses V(t)=e −t/T₂ . T1 describes energy relaxation, while T2 describes decay of transverse coherence. In a simple Markovian qubit model, 1/T2=1/(2T1)+1/Tφ, where Tφ characterizes pure dephasing. An ensemble of static detunings has visibility equal to the magnitude of its characteristic function ⟨e iΔt ⟩. Gaussian detuning noise produces a Gaussian envelope, not necessarily an exponential. The fitted symbol T2* often includes this inhomogeneous broadening. State the envelope before quoting a coherence time. An ideal π echo reverses the sign of phase accumulation from a constant detuning during the second half. The accumulated phases cancel, while irreversible loss to an inaccessible environment need not. The ability to recover a signal supplies information about the noise; it does not establish that all apparent decoherence is reversible."}, {"title": "14.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#section-14-3", "text": "14.3 What the evidence can establish Fit relaxation and coherence experiments separately. Correct for imperfect pulse contrast, measure enough delays to resolve the envelope, and avoid using an unconstrained exponential fit when the data show revivals or oscillatory noise. Different pulse sequences probe different parts of a noise spectrum. A diagonal reduced density matrix predicts local statistics but does not identify one definite global outcome. The cat chapter returns to that distinction. Here the operational task is narrower: measure how well a phase-sensitive signal survives and what an echo can recover. In the display, the sphere's transverse component shrinks with the chosen envelope. The resulting shorter arrow represents a mixed reduced state, not a smaller physical particle. Pressing play advances delay, while pausing permits direct comparison of the fringe and envelope."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Compare free evolution with the ideal echo mode. Locate one full phase period and explain why the envelope, rather than the individual fringe minima, measures lost contrast. Interactive model · Idealized predictions A clock made from a superposition A clock made from a superposition Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. |0⟩ |1⟩ x y Prepare |0⟩ calibrate readout Controlled evolution pulse / delay Measure populations repeat on fresh systems On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Delay t / T₂ 1 Configuration Free evolution Ideal static-noise echo Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How can phase evolution reveal coherence and its loss? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#worked", "text": "A worked example At t=T2 and Δt=0 modulo 2π, what is P0 for unit initial contrast? P0=(1+e⁻¹)/2≈0.684. At the opposite phase it is about 0.316. The fringe contrast is e⁻¹, not either of these probabilities individually."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 14.1 For T1=100 μs and no pure dephasing, what is T2? Hint Use the stated Markovian relation. Worked solution T2=2T1=200 μs within that model. Problem 14.2 Does an echo restore a particle already lost from the apparatus? Hint Identify what the pulse reverses. Worked solution No. Refocusing phase does not undo particle loss or ordinary irreversible relaxation. Problem 14.3 Why can a Gaussian envelope arise? Hint Average exp(iΔt) over a Gaussian static detuning distribution. Worked solution The average is exp(−σ²t²/2), so static frequency spread gives a Gaussian contrast decay."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Distinguish phase evolution, ensemble averaging, and irreversible noise. A coherence time belongs to a specified model and measurement sequence."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Ramsey, Decoherence, and Echo", "url": "14-ramsey.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Ramsey: Molecular beams, Nobel lecture Schlosshauer: Decoherence, the measurement problem, and interpretations"}, {"title": "15.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#section-15-1", "text": "15.1 The question and the apparatus A z measurement alone cannot distinguish |+⟩ from a balanced mixture of |0⟩ and |1⟩. Both give equal z counts. Prepare many fresh copies of the same procedure and divide them among x, y, and z measurements. The extra bases reveal phase-sensitive information missing from the first dataset. This is state tomography. You do not measure all three components on one untouched system. Each measurement consumes or changes its copy. The procedure assumes that the preparations across the three groups represent the same state and that the measurement axes are calibrated. The explorer varies the polar angle and purity of a state in the xz plane. It shows a Bloch vector and the three predicted plus-outcome probabilities. The restriction y=0 is for legibility; the reconstruction formula itself includes all three components."}, {"title": "15.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#section-15-2", "text": "15.2 Build a prediction Any qubit density matrix can be written \\(\\rho=(I+r_xX+r_yY+r_zZ)/2\\), with a real vector r satisfying |r|≤1. The measured plus probabilities give rk=2P(+k)−1. Pure states lie on the unit sphere; mixed states lie inside. The matrix is \\(\\tfrac12\\begin{pmatrix}1+r_z&r_x-ir_y\\\\r_x+ir_y&1-r_z\\end{pmatrix}\\). Its eigenvalues are (1±|r|)/2, so |r|≤1 is the condition for nonnegative probabilities for every possible measurement. Purity is Trρ²=(1+|r|²)/2. Finite independent samples can produce a linear estimate with |r|>1. This does not discover a new quantum state outside the sphere. It signals statistical or systematic error in an unconstrained estimate. Physical reconstruction methods impose positivity and should report uncertainties rather than silently clipping every component separately."}, {"title": "15.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#section-15-3", "text": "15.3 What the evidence can establish Tomography is only as trustworthy as its preparation and measurement assumptions. If every axis is miscalibrated consistently, a reconstruction can look excellent and still describe the wrong physical operations. Self-consistent calibration and independent validation measurements address different parts of that problem. Do not confuse an estimated density matrix with a unique preparation history. The maximally mixed state has many ensemble decompositions. Tomography predicts measurement statistics and cannot determine which decomposition was used without additional records. The browser gives exact model probabilities, so the vector always remains physical. Use the exercises to examine noisy estimates. A practical tomography report should give the counts per setting, the measurement model, the fitting method, and a check against held-out settings."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Compare a pure equatorial state with a zero-length Bloch vector. Their z probabilities match, but their x probabilities differ. Rotate the pure state and predict all three measurement probabilities. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Measure along three axes Measure along three axes Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. |0⟩ |1⟩ x y X copies separate fresh preparations Y copies separate fresh preparations Z copies separate fresh preparations On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. State polar angle (degrees) 60 Configuration Pure state Bloch length 0.4 Maximally mixed Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How can several incompatible measurements describe one preparation? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#worked", "text": "A worked example Measured plus frequencies are Px=0.8, Py=0.5, Pz=0.9. Reconstruct the vector and check physicality. r=(0.6,0,0.8), whose length is one. The reconstructed matrix is [[0.9,0.3],[0.3,0.1]]. Its determinant is zero and its trace one, so it is a pure state under the assumed calibration."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 15.1 What is the state if all three plus probabilities are 1/2? Hint Compute r component by component. Worked solution r=0 and ρ=I/2, the maximally mixed qubit state. Problem 15.2 Why is Px=Py=Pz=0.9 not a physical exact qubit prediction? Hint Calculate |r|. Worked solution r=(0.8,0.8,0.8) has length √1.92>1, giving a negative eigenvalue in the linear reconstruction. Problem 15.3 Can tomography distinguish different ensembles with the same density matrix? Hint Use the Born rule. Worked solution No system-only measurement distinguishes identical density matrices. Extra classical preparation records may distinguish their histories."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Tomography reconstructs a statistical state from calibrated measurements on repeated preparations. Positivity and uncertainty are part of the inference."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Reconstructing a Quantum State", "url": "15-tomography.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. James et al.: Measurement of qubits"}, {"title": "16.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#section-16-1", "text": "16.1 The question and the apparatus Send one photon into each input of a balanced beam splitter and count coincidences between its outputs. If the photons overlap in their relevant modes, coincidence counts are suppressed: the Hong–Ou–Mandel effect. Delaying one photon makes them distinguishable and restores the baseline. Overlap includes timing, spectrum, polarization, and spatial mode. Equal colour labels alone do not guarantee indistinguishability. A delay scan is useful because it changes one of those variables while leaving much of the apparatus fixed. Loss, multiphoton emissions, and detector response still affect the measured dip. The visual shows two incoming wave-packet envelopes, their relative delay, and a coincidence curve. It depicts the cancellation of two-photon alternatives, not an attraction between photons. In the ideal output state, either both photons leave one port or both leave the other."}, {"title": "16.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#section-16-2", "text": "16.2 Build a prediction With a consistent balanced splitter convention, input creation operators transform as a†→(c†+d†)/√2 and b†→(c†−d†)/√2. Their product becomes [(c†)²−(d†)²]/2. The c†d† terms cancel. Properly normalized, the output is (|2,0⟩−|0,2⟩)/√2. For pure single-photon modes f and g, the coincidence probability is \\(P_c=(1-|\\langle f|g\\rangle|^2)/2\\). The teaching model chooses squared overlap exp(−δ²), where δ is dimensionless delay. This gives Pc=0 at perfect overlap and approaches 1/2 at large separation. Entanglement is a related but distinct property. A polarization pair such as (|HH⟩+|VV⟩)/√2 has correlations that cannot be represented as a product state. Comparing several polarization bases distinguishes it from an ordinary mixture of HH and VV. A Hong–Ou–Mandel dip alone does not certify every desired polarization-entanglement property."}, {"title": "16.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#section-16-3", "text": "16.3 What the evidence can establish Measure the large-delay baseline and the near-zero-delay coincidence rate using the same normalization. Report the source model and how multiphoton terms were handled. Visibility can be affected by postselection, so the protocol must say which trials enter the denominator. The two-photon experiment tests indistinguishability through interference. Entanglement certification requires an appropriate witness, tomography, or a Bell test with its assumptions. Different optical experiments reveal different resources; a single impressive plot is not a complete characterization. In this course the dip is an ideal prediction. The next chapters use entangled states to examine local models and contextual value assignments. Their conclusions require more than mode overlap: measurement settings, correlations, and assumptions about which values can be jointly assigned become central."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Slide one packet through the other. Compare the temporal overlap with the coincidence dip. Explain why the large-delay coincidence probability is 1/2 rather than one. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Overlap two photons Overlap two photons Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Photon A mode f Photon B mode g, delay δ Detector 0 Detector 1 Coincidences test mode overlap. On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Relative delay 0 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table What changes when two indistinguishable photons meet? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#worked", "text": "A worked example If the squared mode overlap is 0.8, what coincidence probability is predicted? Pc=(1−0.8)/2=0.1. Relative to the distinguishable baseline 0.5, the ideal dip visibility is (0.5−0.1)/0.5=0.8."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 16.1 Why is the coincidence baseline 1/2 for distinguishable inputs? Hint List the independent output alternatives. Worked solution Each input has two equally likely outputs. Two of the four alternatives put one photon at each output. Problem 16.2 Does a pair that always gives matching H/V results have to be entangled? Hint Compare a mixture of HH and VV. Worked solution No. A 50/50 classical mixture gives the same H/V correlations. Measurements in other bases are needed. Problem 16.3 What happens if the photons have orthogonal polarizations? Hint Consider their mode inner product. Worked solution Without erasing that distinguishability, the overlap is zero and the ideal coincidence probability is 1/2."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Two-photon interference probes indistinguishability. Entanglement and nonlocal correlations require their own measurements and criteria."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Two-Photon Interference and Entanglement", "url": "16-two-photon.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Hong, Ou and Mandel: Measurement of subpicosecond time intervals between two photons"}, {"title": "17.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#section-17-1", "text": "17.1 The question and the apparatus The EPR argument asks whether quantum mechanics completely describes physical reality. Perfect correlations appear to let one predict a distant quantity without acting on the distant system. Bohm's spin version makes this accessible with two spin-one-half particles in a singlet. Equal-axis measurements always give opposite results, while either local record alone is random. Perfect anticorrelation is not sufficient to rule out a classical account: a source could send opposite prewritten instructions. Bell's insight is to compare several independently chosen measurement settings and constrain the entire family of correlations predicted by a local model. Each station records its setting and a result ±1. The visual uses singlet spins measured along axes in one plane. Alice's axes are 0° and 90°. Bob's axes are θ and −θ. This is a spin-angle convention; photon polarization correlations involve doubled physical analyzer angles. Keeping the convention explicit avoids a common factor-of-two error."}, {"title": "17.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#section-17-2", "text": "17.2 Build a prediction A local model with shared variable λ factorizes the conditional probabilities as P(a,b|x,y,λ)=P(a|x,λ)P(b|y,λ). Assume the setting distribution is independent of λ. Deterministic assignments suffice for deriving the bound because stochastic assignments are mixtures of them. For predetermined ±1 values A0,A1,B0,B1, the expression C=A0(B0+B1)+A1(B0−B1) is always ±2: one bracket vanishes and the other is ±2. Averaging yields \\(|S|=|E_{00}+E_{01}+E_{10}-E_{11}|\\le2\\). This is the CHSH inequality. Quantum singlet correlations obey E(a,b)=−cos(a−b). With the axes in the visual, S=−2(cosθ+sinθ), reaching magnitude 2√2 at θ=45°. The local marginals remain 1/2, so stronger correlations do not enable a controllable message from one station to the other."}, {"title": "17.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#section-17-3", "text": "17.3 What the evidence can establish A physical Bell test must address detection and locality loopholes and account for the setting process and statistical analysis. Throwing away missing detections can invalidate the simple local bound unless justified by an additional sampling assumption. A finite estimate slightly above two needs a statistical analysis; the curve in the simulator is an exact model expectation. A violation rules out the stated package of local factorization, setting independence, and the experimental assumptions. It is not a proof that communication can exceed light speed, that every notion of reality is impossible, or that one interpretation is uniquely correct. Different interpretations locate the failed assumptions differently. The lesson separates EPR's motivation, the Bell inequality, and its experimental test. The thought argument alone is not a measured violation. The browser implements the quantum correlation function, so seeing it cross the bound checks the model and geometry, not nature."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Sweep Bob’s angle and find the largest |S|. Inspect the four correlation terms separately. Check that the local outcome probabilities stay balanced throughout. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Four correlations, one bound Four correlations, one bound Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Singlet source local marginals 1/2 Alice a₀=0°, a₁=90° Bob b₀=θ, b₁=−θ Independent settings · outcomes ±1 Compare records to estimate four correlations. On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Bob angle θ (degrees) 45 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Which assumptions about separated systems can correlations test? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#worked", "text": "A worked example At θ=45°, calculate the four correlations and S. E00=E01=E10=−1/√2 and E11=+1/√2. Thus S=−4/√2=−2√2, whose magnitude exceeds the local bound two."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 17.1 Why can perfect anticorrelation at one setting have a local explanation? Hint Construct opposite instructions. Worked solution The source can send A=λ and B=−λ for a random λ=±1. Additional settings are needed to constrain such models. Problem 17.2 What is |S| at θ=0°? Hint Substitute into the derived expression. Worked solution |S|=2, at the local bound. Entanglement alone does not make every setting choice violate CHSH. Problem 17.3 Can Alice choose her individual singlet outcome to signal? Hint Distinguish a setting choice from a random result. Worked solution No. Her outcome is uncontrollable and Bob’s marginal remains balanced. Correlations are revealed by comparing records."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Bell tests constrain a precise assumption package using correlations across settings. Keep the local bound, quantum prediction, and statistical estimate distinct."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "EPR, Bohm, and Bell Tests", "url": "17-epr-bell.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Aspect: Experimental tests of Bell’s inequalities, a first-hand account Hensen et al.: Loophole-free Bell inequality violation"}, {"title": "18.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#section-18-1", "text": "18.1 The question and the apparatus Prepare three qubits in |GHZ⟩=(|000⟩+|111⟩)/√2 and let each station choose X or Y. Rather than estimate a smooth correlation curve, focus on products of three outcomes. Quantum theory predicts certain products for selected triples of settings. Those certainties conflict with a single assignment of all six local values. A second construction, the Peres–Mermin square, uses compatible observables arranged into rows and columns. Each observable appears in two contexts. Its purpose is to test the assumption that its assigned value is independent of which compatible companions are measured. Spatial separation and measurement compatibility are different physical conditions. The explorer switches between the GHZ product ledger and the square. Step through the rows of the argument. This is an animated logical calculation, not a simulated loophole-free experiment. Each displayed product must be tied to the specified quantum state or operator identity."}, {"title": "18.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#section-18-2", "text": "18.2 Build a prediction For the chosen GHZ state, the predictions are XXX=+1, XYY=−1, YXY=−1, and YYX=−1. Suppose all local X and Y results have preassigned ±1 values independent of the other stations' choices. Multiply the last three equalities: each Y value appears twice and cancels, leaving XXX=−1, contradicting the first prediction. For the square use rows (XI, IX, XX), (IY, YI, YY), and (XY, YX, ZZ), with tensor products implied. Each row contains commuting observables and has product +I. The first two columns also have product +I, while the last column has product −I. These are operator identities, independent of the state. A context-independent assignment respecting these product relations is impossible: multiplying all six row and column constraints gives +1 from each value appearing twice, but −1 from the prescribed products. The inference uses compatibility and functional consistency. It does not merely say that all unperformed questions are meaningless."}, {"title": "18.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#section-18-3", "text": "18.3 What the evidence can establish Ideal certainty proofs become statistical tests when there is noise. For GHZ, the Mermin combination M=⟨XXX⟩−⟨XYY⟩−⟨YXY⟩−⟨YYX⟩ is four ideally, while deterministic local assignments have |M|≤2. A visibility model scales the ideal correlations, giving a violation only above the corresponding threshold. Contextuality experiments must check compatibility and disturbance. Measuring purportedly compatible observables with different apparatus implementations can introduce a context dependence unrelated to the intended theorem. The operational version of a noncontextuality assumption should be stated before interpreting a violation. These examples sharpen the distinction between a mathematical contradiction in an assignment scheme and a contradiction in quantum predictions. Quantum theory consistently computes each compatible measurement context. The conflict comes from demanding one joint classical value table satisfying every constraint."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Switch between GHZ and the Peres–Mermin square. Step through each constraint and multiply the signs by hand. Identify which values occur twice and which product carries the negative sign. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Follow the product constraints Follow the product constraints Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. GHZ source |000⟩ + |111⟩ Alice choose X or Y Bob choose X or Y Charlie choose X or Y XXX: +1     XYY: −1     YXY: −1     YYX: −1 Multiply the last three constraints. On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Argument step 0 Configuration GHZ correlations Peres–Mermin square Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Can every possible measurement have a context-independent value? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#worked", "text": "A worked example If every ideal GHZ correlation is reduced by visibility v, when does the Mermin expression exceed two? M=4v, so a violation requires v>1/2 in this specific white-noise-style scaling model. At v=1/2 it reaches the bound; experimental uncertainty still matters."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 18.1 Why do the Y values disappear in the GHZ multiplication? Hint Each takes a value ±1. Worked solution Every local Y value appears twice, and (±1)²=1. Problem 18.2 Does the Peres–Mermin square require a special entangled input state? Hint Inspect whether the constraints are state expectations or operator identities. Worked solution The product identities hold for every state in the two-qubit space. Implementing compatible measurements remains essential. Problem 18.3 Why is checking disturbance important in a contextuality test? Hint Compare the same observable in different contexts. Worked solution If its implementation or preceding operations change it, the measured contexts may not represent the same observable assumed in the argument."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward A no-go argument identifies incompatible assumptions. Real tests also need the compatibility, calibration, and statistical conditions behind the ideal algebra."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "GHZ and Contextuality", "url": "18-ghz-contextuality.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Mermin: Simple unified form for the major no-hidden-variables theorems"}, {"title": "19.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#section-19-1", "text": "19.1 The question and the apparatus Alice holds an unknown qubit and one half of a Bell pair; Bob holds the other half. Alice performs an entangling operation and measures her two qubits. She sends two classical bits to Bob, who applies a correction. The protocol transfers the input state to Bob's system while consuming the shared entanglement. The transferred object is a state, not matter. Alice does not learn its arbitrary amplitudes from one copy and does not retain an independent copy. The two-bit message is necessary: without it, Bob cannot know which correction to apply and cannot recover a controllable signal. The visual lets you choose a real input state and one of the four measurement branches. It displays Bob's state before and after the correction. A branch selector examines conditional predictions; it is not a way for Alice to choose her random measurement outcomes."}, {"title": "19.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#section-19-2", "text": "19.2 Build a prediction Write |ψ⟩=α|0⟩+β|1⟩, with |α|²+|β|²=1. After Alice's CNOT and Hadamard, the joint state can be grouped as \\(\\tfrac12\\sum_{a,b=0}^1|ab\\rangle X^bZ^a|\\psi\\rangle\\). Each branch has probability 1/4, independent of the input. Bob applies ZᵃXᵇ, the inverse up to an irrelevant global phase. Before the message arrives, Bob averages over the four branches: ρB=¼ΣabXᵇZᵃρZᵃXᵇ=I/2. This explains the absence of faster-than-light transmission within the protocol. The dependence on |ψ⟩ appears in correlations with Alice's classical record. Entanglement swapping replaces the unknown input with one member of another Bell pair. A Bell measurement on the two middle systems prepares a conditional Bell state of the outer systems. Its label depends on the middle result. This does not mean the outer observers can identify that conditional state from their unsorted local records."}, {"title": "19.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#section-19-3", "text": "19.3 What the evidence can establish A laboratory claim must specify the ensemble of input states, success probability, heralding, and the definition of fidelity. The familiar average fidelity benchmark 2/3 applies to deterministic measure-and-prepare strategies for a uniformly distributed unknown pure qubit. It is not a universal benchmark for arbitrary selected inputs or postselected protocols. Linear-optical Bell measurements can have limited success probabilities, so a high conditional fidelity must be reported with the successful-event rate. Check that the state was not simply known in advance to the receiving apparatus. Input selection and verification measurements are part of the experiment. The explorer is an exact ideal circuit identity with real amplitudes. General complex inputs obey the same identity. Its branch-by-branch agreement verifies the algebra but does not measure transmission fidelity, loss tolerance, or the quality of an actual entanglement source."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Choose an input angle, cycle through all four messages, and apply the correction. Compare the corrected state with the input. Then average the uncorrected branches conceptually to recover I/2. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Four messages, one recovered state Four messages, one recovered state Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. |0⟩ |1⟩ x y Alice: Bell measurement two classical bits Bob: conditional state Xᵇ Zᵃ |ψ⟩ Apply the correction Zᵃ Xᵇ → |ψ⟩ On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Input polar angle (degrees) 60 Configuration Message 00 Message 01 Message 10 Message 11 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table What is transferred when a quantum state is teleported? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#worked", "text": "A worked example If Alice records a=1,b=0, what state does Bob have and how does he correct it? Bob has Z|ψ⟩=α|0⟩−β|1⟩. Applying Z again restores |ψ⟩ because Z²=I. The message identifies the correction without revealing α or β."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 19.1 Why are the two classical bits insufficient to describe an arbitrary qubit by themselves? Hint Compare the continuous input parameters with four messages. Worked solution The bits label a correction to a pre-existing entangled resource. They are not a classical specification of the continuous amplitudes. Problem 19.2 What state does Bob assign without the message? Hint Average all four Pauli branches. Worked solution I/2 for every input, so no input information can be recovered locally before receiving the message. Problem 19.3 Does postselected fidelity alone characterize a useful experiment? Hint Consider an almost never successful device. Worked solution No. Report success probability, input ensemble, loss, and the applicable classical comparison alongside conditional fidelity."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Teleportation combines entanglement, a local measurement, and classical communication. Conditional state transfer does not bypass communication or clone the input."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Teleportation and Entanglement Swapping", "url": "19-teleportation.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Bennett et al.: Teleporting an unknown quantum state"}, {"title": "20.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#section-20-1", "text": "20.1 The question and the apparatus Imagine a microscopic trigger coupled to a detector and then to a macroscopic record. Schrödinger's cat makes the consequences vivid by replacing a pointer with two macroscopically distinct cat conditions. No animal experiment is needed: the thought experiment asks what follows if the same linear dynamics describes the trigger, detector, and record. A reliable interaction maps |0⟩|ready⟩ to |0⟩|record 0⟩ and |1⟩|ready⟩ to |1⟩|record 1⟩. Linearity then maps a superposition of inputs into a superposition of correlated records. The interaction explains why each term contains a consistent record. It does not, by itself, select one term as the sole outcome. The visual uses two record boxes rather than a literal animated animal. Their weights are branch probabilities in the model, not measured percentages of a partly living organism. A coherence control compares the full coherent description with the reduced statistics available after environmental information is ignored."}, {"title": "20.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#section-20-2", "text": "20.2 Build a prediction For a balanced trigger, the joint pure state is \\(|\\Psi\\rangle=(|0,R_0\\rangle+|1,R_1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\). An incoherent mixture assigns probability 1/2 to each product state. Both predict the same pointer-basis counts, but they differ on suitable joint interference measurements. In the two-dimensional subspace spanned by |0,R0⟩ and |1,R1⟩, write ρ=½[[1,γ],[γ*,1]]. The pure balanced state has γ=1 and the fully dephased mixture has γ=0. Measuring in the joint ± basis gives P+=(1+Reγ)/2. Thus equal record frequencies do not imply identical global states. If the environment stores states |E0⟩ and |E1⟩, the accessible coherence is multiplied by ⟨E1|E0⟩. Nearly orthogonal environment states suppress interference in the reduced description. The formal operation of tracing out the environment is not an additional law that chooses one unique global result."}, {"title": "20.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#section-20-3", "text": "20.3 What the evidence can establish The measurement problem concerns how to connect the formal description to definite experienced outcomes. Interpretations and modified dynamics address it differently. Decoherence explains why interference between certain records becomes locally inaccessible and why stable pointer descriptions are useful, but it does not alone select an interpretation. Laboratory cat states are coherent superpositions of distinguishable states in controlled systems, such as field modes or collective degrees of freedom. Calling them cats does not mean that a living animal's conscious state has been coherently controlled. Assess the actual Hilbert space, separation, and coherence witness used in a claim. The diagram's branches are a bookkeeping tool shared by the calculation. It neither declares multiple worlds real nor assumes an objective collapse. Read it with an assumption ledger: linear evolution, the meaning of a state, and the account of outcomes are separate commitments."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Reduce coherence while watching the record probabilities remain 1/2 each. Compare the joint + measurement, which changes, with the pointer counts, which do not. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Records and coherence Records and coherence Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Prepare + system Isolated laboratory |0, record 0⟩ weight 1/2 |1, record 1⟩ weight 1/2 γ Outside read / interfere The two records are schematic macroscopic alternatives. On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Coherence γ 1 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Does a correlated wavefunction describe one observed outcome? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#worked", "text": "A worked example A reduced record subspace has real γ=0.4. What is P+ and how does it compare with pointer statistics? P+=(1+0.4)/2=0.7. The two pointer outcomes remain 0.5 each. Interference measurements distinguish states that pointer counts alone cannot."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 20.1 Does a diagonal reduced state prove a global collapse happened? Hint Compare a partial trace with a dynamical change. Worked solution No. Entanglement with an ignored environment can give the same local diagonal matrix while the global state remains pure. Problem 20.2 What is the difference between a coherent state sum and a classical mixture here? Hint Inspect the off-diagonal terms. Worked solution The coherent sum includes cross terms that affect joint interference. The mixture lacks those terms even though pointer frequencies agree. Problem 20.3 Why should experimental cat claims specify the degrees of freedom? Hint The metaphor covers very different physical systems. Worked solution The preparation and coherence witness apply to a particular system, such as two oscillator wave packets. They do not automatically establish coherent control over all macroscopic or biological variables."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Correlation, loss of accessible interference, and a unique outcome are different explanatory tasks. The cat makes their separation explicit."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Schrödinger’s Cat and the Measurement Problem", "url": "20-schrodinger-cat.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Schlosshauer: Decoherence, the measurement problem, and interpretations Haroche: Controlling photons in a box and exploring the quantum to classical boundary"}, {"title": "21.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#section-21-1", "text": "21.1 The question and the apparatus A friend inside an isolated laboratory measures a qubit prepared in |+⟩. The friend records zero or one. Wigner outside treats the qubit and the friend’s record as a combined quantum system. If the interaction is unitary and the whole laboratory remains isolated, Wigner writes a coherent superposition of the two correlated record states. Two possible later actions must be distinguished. Wigner can open the laboratory and read the record, or attempt a joint interference measurement that probes coherence between the entire record branches. The second action requires extraordinary control, including every degree of freedom that contains which-record information. It is not equivalent to asking the friend what happened. The visual models the friend as a reversible memory qubit. That makes the algebra explicit and experimentally approachable, but does not model consciousness or prove that a human observer can be coherently reversed. The sealed box is an assumption in the thought experiment, not evidence that macroscopic isolation is achievable."}, {"title": "21.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#section-21-2", "text": "21.2 Build a prediction Let |F0⟩ and |F1⟩ be orthogonal memory states. Ideal premeasurement gives |Ψ⟩=(|0,F0⟩+|1,F1⟩)/√2. Reading the record yields equal probabilities. Define |W±⟩=(|0,F0⟩±|1,F1⟩)/√2. Wigner's coherent description predicts P(W+)=1; an actual incoherent 50/50 mixture predicts 1/2. A convenient circuit writes a record with a CNOT from system to memory. Reversing that CNOT, then measuring the system in X, implements an interference test on this ideal subspace. It also erases the memory's record. One cannot both retain an independent perfectly distinguishing record and recover perfect interference in that same reduced system. With real residual coherence γ, the explorer gives P(W+)=(1+γ)/2. The friend's record probabilities remain 1/2. A state conditioned on a particular friend's record and Wigner's unconditioned joint state answer different questions. They cannot be substituted into one another without tracking the conditioning and the later measurement."}, {"title": "21.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#section-21-3", "text": "21.3 What the evidence can establish Wigner's friend exposes the difficulty of applying quantum theory to observers who themselves use it. The tension depends on what a quantum state represents, how outcome statements are used, and which global operations are allowed. Different interpretations respond differently. The elementary calculation is not a proof that ordinary quantum predictions are inconsistent. Experiments can implement memory systems and interference operations inspired by the scenario. Their conclusions concern those systems and their measured correlations. Equating such memories with conscious observers is an additional interpretive step, not an observed feature of the apparatus. The essential practical limitation is leaked information. If an environment holds an unreversed copy of the record, reversing the small memory alone does not restore full coherence. Any claim of a Wigner measurement should identify the controlled subsystem and explain which records remain outside it."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Switch Wigner’s measurement between reading the record and testing interference. Vary the surviving coherence. Observe that the two measurement choices reveal different properties of the same joint-state model. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Inside the laboratory; outside the laboratory Inside the laboratory; outside the laboratory Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Prepare + system Isolated laboratory |0, record 0⟩ weight 1/2 |1, record 1⟩ weight 1/2 γ Outside read / interfere On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Coherence γ 1 Configuration Wigner tests interference Wigner reads the record Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Can an observer and an outside experimenter assign different states consistently? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#worked", "text": "A worked example A record is copied to an inaccessible environment with orthogonal states. Wigner reverses only the system–memory CNOT. What X statistics remain? The system is still correlated with orthogonal environment records. Its reduced state is I/2, so both X outcomes have probability 1/2. Reversing the accessible record does not erase the environmental copy."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 21.1 What does Wigner predict for reading the friend’s record in the coherent model? Hint Use the record basis, not the W basis. Worked solution Zero and one each have probability 1/2. Coherence is not visible in that basis. Problem 21.2 What distinguishes a coherent laboratory from a mixture? Hint Find a joint observable with different expectations. Worked solution The W± measurement gives P(W+)=1 for the ideal coherent state and 1/2 for the mixture. Problem 21.3 Does a photonic memory experiment show that a person’s experience was reversed? Hint Identify the implemented observer model. Worked solution No. It tests controlled quantum degrees of freedom used as a record. A claim about conscious experience requires assumptions beyond those measurements."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Specify the observer, the conditioning, and the later measurement. A reversible memory model illuminates the thought experiment without settling the status of human observers."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Wigner’s Friend", "url": "21-wigners-friend.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Bong et al.: A strong no-go theorem on the Wigner’s friend paradox Deutsch: Quantum theory as a universal physical theory"}, {"title": "22.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#section-22-1", "text": "22.1 The question and the apparatus Extended Wigner scenarios place more than one observing laboratory inside a larger coherent experiment. Frauchiger and Renner analyse a protocol in which agents reason about other agents' predictions. Bong and colleagues formulate a different family of constraints, called local-friendliness inequalities. These are related questions, but they are not the same theorem. We first examine a two-laboratory state that captures the probability calculation in the Frauchiger–Renner setup. Each laboratory includes a recorded result. Treat the states |h⟩ and |t⟩ as orthogonal records in the first lab, and |d⟩ and |u⟩ as orthogonal records in the second. An outside experimenter can, ideally, measure superpositions of those record states. The display separates record-basis probabilities from probabilities in the two outside ok/fail bases. It animates the three amplitude contributions to an ok,ok event. No agents' beliefs are simulated. The inference rules must be examined in words rather than inferred from the animation alone."}, {"title": "22.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#section-22-2", "text": "22.2 Build a prediction Consider \\(|\\Psi\\rangle=(|h,d\\rangle+|t,d\\rangle+|t,u\\rangle)/\\sqrt3\\). Let |ok⟩A=(|h⟩−|t⟩)/√2 and |ok⟩B=(|d⟩−|u⟩)/√2, with plus signs defining fail. The three contributions to the ok,ok amplitude are +1/√12, −1/√12, and +1/√12. Their sum is 1/√12, so the probability is 1/12. The other joint probabilities are P(ok,fail)=1/12, P(fail,ok)=1/12, and P(fail,fail)=3/4. They sum to one. Ordinary unitary quantum theory therefore makes an unambiguous prediction for this fully specified joint measurement. The conceptual puzzle adds certainty inferences: conditioned on t, the second lab is in the fail superposition; a recorded u excludes h; and an outside ok in the first lab excludes d in the relevant hybrid context. Chaining those statements across observers can appear to forbid ok,ok. The chain crosses different measurement contexts and uses assumptions about transferable certainty and single outcomes. The nonzero amplitude alone is not a contradiction."}, {"title": "22.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#section-22-3", "text": "22.3 What the evidence can establish Frauchiger–Renner's no-go claim concerns a package of universal quantum reasoning, consistency between agents, and single outcomes under its protocol. Responses differ over which inference steps are warranted. This chapter supplies the finite amplitude calculation and locates the extra reasoning assumptions; it does not pretend that a probability table proves a unique interpretation. Local-friendliness results instead constrain combinations including absoluteness of observed events and local causal assumptions, under idealized control of friend systems. Their experimental implementations use small quantum systems as observers. Extending the conclusion to conscious observers or unrestricted macroscopic laboratories requires further premises. In both cases, specify what is physically measured, what is counterfactual, and what information each agent conditions on. When a proposal uses an interference measurement on a laboratory, retaining its original record elsewhere may change the prediction. A source's theorem statement and operational scenario should be read together."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Switch between the record and outside bases. In the outside basis, step through the three signed contributions to ok,ok. Square their sum, not their individual magnitudes, and compare with the probability table. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Add signed amplitudes Add signed amplitudes Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. |h,d⟩ amplitude 1/√3 +1/√12 |t,d⟩ amplitude 1/√3 −1/√12 |t,u⟩ amplitude 1/√3 +1/√12 ok, ok sum, then square (+1 − 1 + 1)² / 12 Probability = 1/12 On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Contribution step 3 Configuration Outside ok/fail basis Friend record basis Play Step Reset Read the values as a table Which assumptions make different observers’ certainty claims conflict? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#worked", "text": "A worked example What erroneous probability results from adding the three squared ok,ok contributions instead of amplitudes? Each squared contribution is 1/12, so the erroneous sum is 1/4. The correct amplitude sum includes cancellation and gives 1/12. Treating alternatives as an incoherent mixture changes the preparation."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 22.1 Check the normalization of the outside probability table. Hint Use a denominator of twelve. Worked solution 1/12+1/12+1/12+9/12=1. Problem 22.2 Does a nonzero ok,ok probability itself make quantum theory inconsistent? Hint Separate the prediction from the additional inference chain. Worked solution No. It is a consistent Born-rule prediction. The tension arises when it is combined with cross-agent certainty claims under an additional assumption package. Problem 22.3 Why are local-friendliness inequalities not interchangeable with the Frauchiger–Renner argument? Hint Compare their premises and experimental tasks. Worked solution They constrain different assumption packages and use different operational scenarios. A violation or inference in one is not automatically a demonstration of the other."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Calculate the specified experiment first. Then identify the extra assumptions needed to transfer claims between observers and incompatible contexts."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Extended Friends and Consistency", "url": "22-extended-friends.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Frauchiger and Renner: Quantum theory cannot consistently describe the use of itself Bong et al.: A strong no-go theorem on the Wigner’s friend paradox"}, {"title": "23.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#section-23-1", "text": "23.1 The question and the apparatus Prepare a superposition, let an unknown frequency offset act for time t, and convert the accumulated phase into a population. Repeat and infer the offset. This is the basic logic behind Ramsey-based frequency sensing. Atomic clocks add a feedback loop that adjusts an oscillator using the atomic response. Sensitivity depends on the slope of the response and the measurement noise. A high-contrast signal at its maximum can be locally insensitive because the slope there is zero. A phase bias moves the operating point to the steep part of a fringe. The apparatus must also resolve which fringe is being measured. The explorer varies the interrogation time in units of a chosen coherence time. It displays the decreasing contrast and the predicted frequency uncertainty for a fixed number of shots. Longer interrogation is beneficial only until the loss of contrast outweighs the additional phase accumulation."}, {"title": "23.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#section-23-2", "text": "23.2 Build a prediction Use P0=[1+V cos(δωt+φ)]/2 and choose the quadrature point where the cosine is zero. There |∂P0/∂δω|=Vt/2. For N independent binary outcomes, the standard error of the measured probability is 1/(2√N). Linear error propagation gives \\(\\delta\\omega\\simeq1/(Vt\\sqrt N)\\). For V=e −t/T₂ and fixed N, minimizing the predicted uncertainty gives t=T2. If instead the total free-evolution time is fixed and overhead is negligible, N≈Ttotal/t and the optimum becomes t=T2/2. The resource constraint changes the answer. Real overhead, correlated noise, and phase ambiguity modify both estimates. Entanglement can change sensitivity scaling in ideal models, but noise and preparation cost must be counted. A claim of quantum advantage requires comparison at matched resources, not merely comparing an entangled sensor with an arbitrarily weak baseline. The present display uses independent probes and makes no entanglement advantage claim."}, {"title": "23.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#section-23-3", "text": "23.3 What the evidence can establish Calibrate the relation between field and frequency, characterize dead time and drift, and report how uncertainty was estimated. Repeated measurements in a noisy laboratory may be correlated, invalidating a naïve √N improvement. Clock stability over time and the uncertainty of an absolute frequency are related but distinct quantities. The displayed optimum is conditional on exponential contrast loss and a fixed-shot budget. It is not an equipment recommendation or a universal optimal sensing time. Use the alternate resource calculation as an example of how experimental design changes when costs are accounted for. A useful extension is to compare the model with a supplied measured Ramsey scan or clock dataset, keeping calibration and uncertainty metadata. Until such measurements are provided, generated curves and CSV files in this course remain labelled synthetic."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Sweep t/T2 and locate the minimum fixed-shot uncertainty. Compare it with the decreasing contrast and the increasing phase-accumulation time. Explain why t=0 is uninformative. Interactive model · Idealized predictions Find a useful interrogation time Find a useful interrogation time Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. |0⟩ |1⟩ x y Prepare |0⟩ calibrate readout Controlled evolution pulse / delay Measure populations repeat on fresh systems On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Time t / T₂ 1 Play Step Reset Read the values as a table How does a phase become a precise estimate of a field or frequency? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#worked", "text": "A worked example At t=T2 with N=100, what is δω in units of 1/T2? V=e⁻¹ and √N=10, so δωT2=e/10≈0.272. This is a local error-propagation estimate near quadrature, not a global confidence interval over many phase wraps."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 23.1 How does quadrupling N change the uncertainty under independent noise? Hint Use the square root dependence. Worked solution It halves the uncertainty. Problem 23.2 Why is the top of a fringe a poor local operating point? Hint Differentiate the probability. Worked solution The first derivative with respect to frequency vanishes there, so a small offset produces only a second-order change. Problem 23.3 Why do fixed-shot and fixed-total-time optimizations differ? Hint Count how many shots fit as t changes. Worked solution With fixed total time, longer shots reduce N. That extra resource cost shifts the optimum from T2 to T2/2 in the ideal overhead-free model."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward Sensitivity is a combination of response slope, noise, and resources. Quote an optimum together with the assumptions that make it optimal."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Quantum Sensing and Atomic Clocks", "url": "23-sensing.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. Wineland: Superposition, entanglement, and raising Schrödinger’s cat"}, {"title": "24.1 The question and the apparatus", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#section-24-1", "text": "24.1 The question and the apparatus Your task is to audit a phase scan from a two-output interferometer. The course provides a downloadable synthetic dataset generated by a seeded binomial model. It is explicitly not measured laboratory data. Its purpose is to make the analysis reproducible and to let you discover how a fitted parameter depends on sampling and assumptions. The dataset contains sixteen equally spaced phase settings, 200 trials per setting, and the two output counts. A visibility control changes the generating model. Resetting returns to the initial model and seed. Export the CSV with its metadata before analysing it, so the exact input is preserved. For an experimental extension, replace the synthetic file with a published dataset whose provenance and reuse conditions permit analysis. Record its source, apparatus, calibration, selection rules, and missing-event treatment. Do not quietly relabel the course file as an experiment or use its agreement with the generator as evidence for quantum theory."}, {"title": "24.2 Build a prediction", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#section-24-2", "text": "24.2 Build a prediction Fit P0(φ)=[1+V cosφ]/2 for this known zero-phase-offset toy model. With evenly spaced settings over a full turn, a simple moment estimate is \\(\\hat V=(2/M)\\sum_{j=1}^M (2k_j/N-1)\\cos\\phi_j\\). This follows from the average of cos²φ being 1/2 and the average of cosφ being zero. For independent binomial counts with a common N, the variance of this estimate is \\(16/(M^2N)\\sum_j p_j(1-p_j)\\cos^2\\phi_j\\). The display uses fitted probabilities for a plug-in standard error. It is an approximation, especially near boundaries; a likelihood analysis or parametric bootstrap is a useful extension. A more realistic fit would include a phase offset, imbalance, and calibrated background. Each added parameter needs enough data to constrain it. Do not force an invalid estimate into [0,1] without reporting that the unconstrained model failed its physical bounds."}, {"title": "24.3 What the evidence can establish", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#section-24-3", "text": "24.3 What the evidence can establish Write a report with a question, apparatus/model diagram, data provenance, method, result with uncertainty, residual plot, alternative explanations, and limitations. Include the CSV and enough code or equations to reproduce the result. State whether the analysis was chosen before looking at the data. Your central conclusion should concern an estimated interference contrast under an explicit binomial model. It should not claim nonclassical light, a Bell violation, or a test of observers: those require different preparations and measurements. The ability to say what a dataset does not establish is an experimental skill. Completion criteria: reproduce the plotted counts; derive or implement the estimator; assess whether residuals show a phase-dependent pattern; compare the estimate with the generating value without hiding discrepancies; and explain how your inference would change with loss, phase drift, or correlated trials. For a real dataset, replace knowledge of the generator with independent calibration and a documented model comparison."}, {"title": "Explore the experiment", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#explore", "text": "Explore the experiment Generate a scan, export it, and reproduce the visibility estimate. Change the generating visibility and see how the estimated uncertainty changes. Compare the synthetic samples with the fitted curve rather than assuming exact agreement. Interactive model · Synthetic observations Audit a synthetic phase scan Audit a synthetic phase scan Schematic apparatus; geometry and moving indicators are explanatory, not measured trajectories. Generate seed + model Estimate visibility ± error Audit residuals + limits A reproducible investigation 16 phases × 200 trials · synthetic data On a small screen, swipe the diagram to inspect its components. Moving marks illustrate the model; they are not measured trajectories. Generating visibility 0.7 Play Step Reset Download CSV Read the values as a table Can you turn counts into a reproducible, appropriately limited conclusion? The text and worked examples remain available without JavaScript. Animation starts only when requested; Step gives a motion-free way to follow the model. Enable JavaScript for adjustable controls. The static apparatus, derivation, and worked values above and below provide the same conceptual explanation."}, {"title": "A worked example", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#worked", "text": "A worked example For a noiseless scan with V=0.6, what does the moment estimator return? Here 2pj−1=0.6cosφj. Substitution gives Vhat=(2/M)×0.6×Σcos²φj=0.6 because the sum is M/2. Sampling noise makes a finite-count estimate fluctuate around this value."}, {"title": "Try it yourself", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#practice", "text": "Try it yourself Problem 24.1 Why include both output counts instead of only their difference? Hint Consider normalization and possible missing trials. Worked solution Both counts preserve the number of detected trials and allow the denominator to be checked. An experimental file should additionally report emitted or heralded trials and losses when available. Problem 24.2 What does a sinusoidal residual after fitting suggest? Hint Think of a missing parameter. Worked solution A phase offset or an additional harmonic may be missing from the model. Calibration drift is another possibility; inspect the collection order and apparatus records. Problem 24.3 What should change when replacing the synthetic scan with a real one? Hint List information the generator gave you for free. Worked solution Document provenance, calibration, timing, efficiency, loss, background, and selection. Check independence and model adequacy instead of assuming the binomial generator describes the apparatus."}, {"title": "What to carry forward", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#takeaways", "text": "What to carry forward A reproducible result includes its data, model, uncertainty, and limits. The capstone completes the course by making those commitments inspectable."}, {"title": "Sources and further exploration", "chapter": "Capstone: Audit an Interference Claim", "url": "24-investigation.html#sources", "text": "Sources and further exploration These readings provide historical context, experimental implementations, or deeper derivations. The interactive display above is an educational model, not a reproduction of their measured data. NIST/SEMATECH: Engineering Statistics Handbook, process modelling"}];
